<Base title="Research" description="Damien Lo's research on privacy and memorization in vision large language models.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1 class="page-title">Research</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      My research asks what a model's observable behavior reveals about what it has learned and memorized. I started
      with a practical privacy question about vision-language models, and it has led me toward a broader, more scientific
      one.
    </p>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">Current</p>
      <h2>Privacy in vision-language models</h2>
      <p>
        Vision large language models are increasingly trained on sensitive images, including medical scans. Membership
        inference attacks test whether a specific image was in a model's training data, which makes them a tool for
        privacy auditing.
      </p>
      <p>
        In my honors thesis and in COMPASS, I studied how VLLM outputs shift as an input image is perturbed with Gaussian
        noise, swept finely from near zero to large. Two findings stood out. First, the clearest membership signal often
        appears at very low noise, a regime earlier perturbation attacks skipped. Second, the direction of the signal
        flips between settings: in some models training images respond <em>less</em> to noise than unseen images, in
        others <em>more</em>. COMPASS turns this into an attack that calibrates both the noise level and the direction
        from unlabeled data. It reaches AUC up to 0.88 on LLaVA v1.5-7B and 0.81 on MiniGPT-4. On the HuluMed-32B medical
        model it reaches 0.64 on endoscopy images, where the strongest baseline sits at chance.
      </p>
      <figure>
        <img src="/compass-fig1.png" width="1340" height="545" loading="lazy"
          alt="Two plots from the COMPASS paper. Left: mean KL divergence against normalized Gaussian noise for members and non-members of LLaVA v1.5-7B, across three noise regimes. Right: the member minus non-member divergence gap, with and without Rényi normalization, dipping most sharply in the middle regime." />
        <figcaption class="small muted">
          Perturbation sensitivity of LLaVA v1.5–7B on Flickr pre-training data across normalized Gaussian noise.
          <strong>Left:</strong> mean KL divergence for members and non-members (bands show one standard deviation).
          <strong>Right:</strong> the member–non-member gap, with and without Rényi normalization; the gap is most
          negative in the low-noise Regime II. From the COMPASS paper (Figure 1).
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="wide">
        <img src="/compass-fig2.png" width="1650" height="652" loading="lazy"
          alt="Diagram of the COMPASS attack: a noise-level calibration phase using an unlabeled target set and a known non-member reference set, a shared perturbation scoring operator (perturb, query the VLLM, token-wise divergence, max-k mean), and a per-sample membership decision." />
        <figcaption class="small muted">
          The COMPASS attack. A tuning phase compares how the target set and a known non-member reference set respond
          across noise levels to pick the perturbation magnitude and score direction; a scoring phase then perturbs each
          image, aggregates the largest token-wise divergences, and orients the result into a membership score. From the
          COMPASS paper (Figure 2).
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p class="small muted aside">
        COMPASS grew out of the method developed in my <a href="/publications">honors thesis</a>, which also evaluated MRI
        and microscopy data. The paper is under review at ICLR 2027.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">In progress</p>
      <h2>Scaling laws for membership inference</h2>
      <p>
        I am exploring empirical scaling laws for membership inference attacks. The goal is to predict how well a
        well-resourced attacker would do from low-cost audits a defender can actually run, which would make privacy risk
        measurable for models too large to audit directly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="emerging">
      <p class="eyebrow">Emerging direction</p>
      <h2>Perturbation landscapes and model geometry</h2>
      <p>
        My thesis began to describe memorized samples as sitting in unusually stable regions of a model's response
        landscape, regions that resist small perturbations. I want to make that picture precise. Which geometric or
        dynamical properties of learned representations produce this stability? How does it relate to generalization?
        Can tools from high-dimensional geometry, dynamical systems and statistical physics explain it?
      </p>
      <p>
        My background in physics and engineering, including two years of experimental condensed-matter research, shapes
        how I approach these questions.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
